---
/*
 * The battery in the landing page's lead, drawn here.
 *
 * It replaced 🔋 for the reason the TypeScript mark replaced ⚡: a line that
 * mixes real marks with emoji standing in for marks reads as an accident. The
 * other two are trademarks, copied rather than redrawn, because an
 * approximation of someone else's mark is worse than no mark at all. This one
 * belongs to nobody — "batteries included" is a figure of speech about
 * defaults, not a product — so it is drawn to this page rather than taken from
 * an icon set.
 *
 * The bolt is the whole point of the drawing. The first version was a plain
 * rounded rectangle with a nub: unmistakably a battery and completely inert,
 * which is a lot of ink to spend on saying "battery" beside a sentence that
 * already says it. Knocking a charge bolt out of the body is what makes the
 * mark say the thing the clause means — the defaults are in it and it is ready
 * to go — rather than illustrating the noun.
 *
 * Six alternatives were drawn and rendered beside the incumbent at the lead's
 * real 19.4px before this one was picked, which is the only way to choose
 * between them — none of the differences survive being described. A segmented
 * gauge read as a stack of dashes, an outlined shell with three inner bars read
 * as a document, and a plain rim-and-fill read as a battery and nothing more.
 * The bolt was legible in every one of them.
 *
 * The geometry that makes it survive. Body 12 units wide on a 24 grid, nub 4.5
 * — an earlier draft at 11 and 5 read as a pill with a bump, because at this
 * size a nub under 3px does not register and a rounded rectangle nobody
 * recognizes is a smudge with good proportions. The bolt is inset about 2.9
 * units from each side of the body, and its arms are 2.1 units across, which is
 * 2.2px on the page. Nothing here is thinner than that on purpose: a 1.2-unit
 * gauge slot came out at 1.3px in an earlier draft and was a gray blur rather
 * than a detail.
 *
 * The coordinates are in `BatteryGlyph.astro`, because the deck row on the same
 * page draws this mark too and one geometry cannot disagree with itself. What
 * stays here is everything that is about the lead in particular: the crop, the
 * size, and the color.
 *
 * The viewBox is cropped to the ink — `6 2 12 20` and not `0 0 24 24`. On the
 * square grid this is drawn on, the battery is a tall narrow glyph with half
 * the box width empty, and that empty half was rendering as 6.3px of dead space
 * between the mark and the word after it, on top of the word space. The other
 * two marks paint to all four edges of their own boxes, so the battery was the
 * only one of the three carrying built-in padding. Cropping is what lets one
 * margin in `global.css` apply to all three and mean the same thing.
 *
 * `currentColor`, unlike the two marks beside it. Those are locked to brand
 * color by a trademark license or by what nominative use allows. This one is
 * ours and is free to mean something, so `global.css` points it at the green
 * this site uses where something is settled rather than promised, which is the
 * claim "batteries included" makes. Drawing in `currentColor` rather than in
 * the hex is what lets one drawing take a light and a dark value with nothing
 * theme-aware in this file.
 *
 * aria-hidden, and not a link. "Batteries" is the noun the sentence beside it
 * already uses, so announcing the glyph would only repeat the line.
 *
 * Size and alignment live in `global.css` with the other two, because the three
 * are set against each other rather than one at a time.
 */
import BatteryGlyph from './BatteryGlyph.astro';
---

<span class="battery-mark">
  <svg viewBox="6 2 12 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" fill="currentColor">
    <BatteryGlyph />
  </svg>
</span>
